  "body_mappings": [
    {
      "section": "en",
      "page": 2,
      "bbox": [
        37.0,
        518.4614173228347,
        461.8976377952756,
        597.6614173228347
      ],
      "text": "Before 1840, British postage was levied according to the number of sheets in a letter and the mileage of its carriage. The sum was usually payable on delivery, so the person receiving the correspondence, not the sender, settled it. Prices thus fluctuated from one postal item to the next."
    },
    {
      "section": "en",
      "page": 2,
      "bbox": [
        37.0,
        410.9614173228347,
        461.8976377952756,
        509.9614173228347
      ],
      "text": "In 1837 Rowland Hill published a booklet urging reform. Parliament enacted the campaigner's proposal in 1839, and on 10 January 1840 the Uniform Penny Post began. A letter of up to half an ounce now cost one penny to any destination in Britain, and the fee was prepaid by whoever sent it. One flat-rate charge replaced variation with simplicity."
    },
    {
      "section": "en",
      "page": 2,
      "bbox": [
        37.0,
        303.4614173228347,
        461.8976377952756,
        402.4614173228347
      ],
      "text": "The Penny Black, a one-penny denomination, was released on 1 May 1840, its validity starting on 6 May. Engraved on it was a youthful likeness of Queen Victoria, the reigning monarch. An aggregate of 68,808,000 were printed. The sheets had no holes for separation, so each stamp was cut out individually with scissors."
    },
    {
      "section": "en",
      "page": 2,
      "bbox": [
        37.0,
        195.96141732283468,
        461.8976377952756,
        294.9614173228347
      ],
      "text": "Mailings doubled, from about 80 million items in 1839 to over 169 million in 1840. The design had a drawback. Against black, the red cancelling mark was barely noticeable, lacking contrast, and the ink could be erased, leaving stamps vulnerable to reuse. In February 1841 the Penny Red, cancelled in black ink, arrived to deter tampering and fraud."
    },
    {
      "section": "en",
      "page": 2,
      "bbox": [
        37.0,
        108.26141732283467,
        461.8976377952756,
        187.46141732283468
      ],
      "text": "As the initiator of postage stamps, Britain set a precedent and earned an exemption. Its stamps are nameless, with no country inscribed on them. By international agreement, the sovereign's head suffices for identification, an implicit designation that other countries spell out in print."
    },
    {
      "section": "zh",
      "page": 3,
      "bbox": [
        37.0,
        537.6614173228347,
        461.8976377952756,
        597.6614173228347
      ],
      "text": "1840年以前，英国的邮费是根据一封信件中的纸张数量及其运输的里程来征收的。这笔钱通常是在投递时应支付的，所以，收到信件的人，而非寄信人，结清邮费。价格此种方式波动，从一件邮政物品到下一件。"
    },
    {
      "section": "zh",
      "page": 3,
      "bbox": [
        37.0,
        449.1614173228347,
        461.8976377952756,
        529.1614173228347
      ],
      "text": "1837年，罗兰·希尔出版了一本小册子，呼吁改革。议会于1839年立法通过了这位改革倡导者的提议，并且在1840年1月10日，“统一便士邮资”开始实行。一封重量不超过半盎司的信，现在寄往英国境内任何目的地都只花费一便士，并且费用由寄信的人预付。一种统一收费以简便取代了差异。"
    },
    {
      "section": "zh",
      "page": 3,
      "bbox": [
        37.0,
        360.6614173228347,
        461.8976377952756,
        440.6614173228347
      ],
      "text": "黑便士，一枚面值一便士的邮票，发行于1840年5月1日，它的有效性从5月6日开始。它的上面雕刻着维多利亚女王年轻的肖像，即这位在位的君主。总计有68,808,000枚被印制。这些整版邮票没有用于分离的孔，所以每枚邮票都被单独地用剪刀剪下。"
    },
    {
      "section": "zh",
      "page": 3,
      "bbox": [
        37.0,
        272.1614173228347,
        461.8976377952756,
        352.1614173228347
      ],
      "text": "邮寄量翻了一番，从1839年的约8000万件邮寄物品增至1840年的超过1.69亿件。这一设计有个缺点。在黑色底色上，红色注销印记几乎不显眼，由于缺乏反差，且墨水能被擦除，导致邮票容易被重复使用。1841年2月，用黑色墨水盖销的“红便士”问世，旨在防止篡改与欺诈。"
    },
    {
      "section": "zh",
      "page": 3,
      "bbox": [
        37.0,
        203.66141732283472,
        461.8976377952756,
        263.6614173228347
      ],
      "text": "作为邮票的首创者，英国开创了先例，并赢得一项豁免。英国邮票是无名的，上面没有刻印国名。根据国际协议，（英国的）君主头像足以用于进行识别，这是一种隐含的标识，然而其他国家则需要把国名印在邮票上。"
    }
  ],
